18
00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.879
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, one second.

19
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:18.049
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so this is the second session, of four sessions where we are sort of walking through the, Personal Data Portability Protocol, PDPP for short.

20
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.440
Tim Nunamaker: And…

21
00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:31.290
Tim Nunamaker: I think Anna said it well last week, that what we're really talking about is the language of data portability, and how can we express, what we need to to make data portable on the internet.

22
00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:32.790
Tim Nunamaker: And…

23
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:39.820
Tim Nunamaker: If you want to see the recording from last week, or just read through the session notes, you can find those through the GitHub.

24
00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.719
Tim Nunamaker: If you can't find it, just ping me in Discord, I'm happy to share that.

25
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.140
Tim Nunamaker: And… We talked a little bit last time about

26
00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:58.320
Tim Nunamaker: sort of the framing of PDPP and the overall architecture. Today, we're going to start to get into a little bit more of the technical details, going into how data is modeled.

27
00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:06.859
Tim Nunamaker: And sort of communicated from data source providers, and then how clients can request access to data.

28
00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:18.600
Tim Nunamaker: Next week, we'll get more into how is Access itself modeled and stored, and how are queries served. And then finally, in the fourth week, we will talk about

29
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.560
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of how the protocol is governed and participation in the protocol. And we'll just go through some of the key open questions.

30
00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:30.449
Tim Nunamaker: So, going back to, last week, just to recap.

31
00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:40.560
Tim Nunamaker: The architecture in terms of, sort of, the flow from a client's perspective, is basically standard OAuth. A client

32
00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.029
Tim Nunamaker: Has a token which is bound to

33
00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:51.079
Tim Nunamaker: In our case, a consent artifact with finely modeled data.

34
00:07:51.650 --> 00:08:00.819
Tim Nunamaker: permissions. So, we… we kind of sneak inside of the token something called a rich authorization request payload, which…

35
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:12.410
Tim Nunamaker: pretty much defines exactly what the client is allowed to access. And of course, for that to work, the data needs to be modeled appropriately, and the server needs to know how to, like, work with that and get to the data.

36
00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:21.059
Tim Nunamaker: So… Some of the primitives involved are, how do you actually define a record of data? How do you…

37
00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.650
Tim Nunamaker: create the language for clients to request access, that's what we're going to talk about today. And…

38
00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.520
Tim Nunamaker: Then how do you store the access, and how do you query into that?

39
00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:52.779
Tim Nunamaker: So, drilling a little bit into these three concepts, the record model, a data source manifest, and a selection request, the record model is sort of the core building block. It's… I have a piece of data, whether that's a post from social media, or a transaction from my credit card statement.

40
00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.529
Tim Nunamaker: Or my gaming profile.

41
00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.309
Tim Nunamaker: And that single piece of data is…

42
00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:12.189
Tim Nunamaker: defined as a record in PDPP, and that's kind of where you start to define the schema, or how is data, modeled and expressed, what are the… what are the types of the fields within the data?

43
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.409
Tim Nunamaker: And a manifest is then, how do you take

44
00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.530
Tim Nunamaker: That record, and package it up into

45
00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:33.989
Tim Nunamaker: A collection, which is called a stream, where there can be ordering of records within that collection, where you may want to express particular semantics about records in the collection.

46
00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.259
Tim Nunamaker: For example.

47
00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:52.710
Tim Nunamaker: if you need to know when something… when data was created, here's the specific timestamp field that you would look at. The manifest kind of describes all of that. And then the selection request is not only the syntax for how does a client ask for

48
00:09:52.800 --> 00:10:00.790
Tim Nunamaker: access to data, but the rules that go along with that. And, both the rules for the client and the rules for the server, that's…

49
00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.149
Tim Nunamaker: Conforming with the protocol.

50
00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:08.490
Tim Nunamaker: So this is what a…

51
00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:15.519
Tim Nunamaker: What a stream, like, a very simple stream, might look like, where… or sorry, a record might look like.

52
00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:23.070
Tim Nunamaker: Where the record is defined as part of a collection, called a stream. In this case, it's a collection of messages.

53
00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.299
Tim Nunamaker: And this particular…

54
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:35.930
Tim Nunamaker: record type is pretty simple. It just defines, here's a conversation that this message came from. This was a message from a user, perhaps as opposed to an agent.

55
00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.699
Tim Nunamaker: What did the user say, and when did they say it?

56
00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.560
Tim Nunamaker: So…

57
00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:53.829
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, this basically can just be expressed as JSON in most cases, and you could define the schema for this as JSON schema. And I think it's worth making a distinction between

58
00:10:53.950 --> 00:11:00.150
Tim Nunamaker: maybe when was the record emitted or known within the protocol? That could be…

59
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:18.719
Tim Nunamaker: like, a timestamp called emitted at, versus when was the record itself created. So maybe, I have an email from many years ago, and there's an important timestamp associated with that email, when did I write it, but when did it actually become part of

60
00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.589
Tim Nunamaker: The data source in the protocol.

61
00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.139
Tim Nunamaker: So there… those could be used for different things.

62
00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:34.150
Tim Nunamaker: Some records can carry blobs. So, for example, an email might have images or videos, and…

63
00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:38.140
Tim Nunamaker: Basically, what you would do is you would just,

64
00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.950
Tim Nunamaker: Create a blob separately, and then a reference would ride.

65
00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:48.299
Tim Nunamaker: Would be attached into the record here, and would be resolvable externally.

66
00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:56.710
Tim Nunamaker: So, one of the key design decisions that we've made within PDP is that…

67
00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:00.539
Tim Nunamaker: Unlike some other protocols, for example, in open banking.

68
00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:08.160
Tim Nunamaker: Where you make a request for data, and you get back a response, you're just getting the current snapshot of the data.

69
00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:19.680
Tim Nunamaker: And if you want to understand how data changes over time, or what has changed since the last time you asked, there isn't really an affordance to do that.

70
00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:28.890
Tim Nunamaker: I think Plaid is an interesting example in the market, where they actually moved away from sort of a static point in time

71
00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:41.939
Tim Nunamaker: query model to a more cursor-based, like, streaming-over-time model, and that's the approach that we're taking in PDPP, because we think that that's actually a very valuable

72
00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:50.300
Tim Nunamaker: way to… to expose data. Especially because so much personal data

73
00:12:51.010 --> 00:13:07.860
Tim Nunamaker: kind of decays and is sort of rapidly… it's almost like it's alive. Like, every day you get new emails in your inbox, or every day you're performing more activity on social media. And so, how that's changing over time is really important, versus maybe, like.

74
00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:11.049
Tim Nunamaker: A single snapshot of your data.

75
00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.880
Tim Nunamaker: That could become outdated, fairly quickly.

76
00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:32.520
Tim Nunamaker: So, PDPP, in order to accommodate streams, has a bunch of features and semantics about, for example, if a record is deleted, there might be a tombstone representing that that once existed and no longer exists.

77
00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:41.540
Tim Nunamaker: And I think the other… Yeah, note about this is, if you're granting standing consent to data, so…

78
00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.600
Tim Nunamaker: I want to give a one-time

79
00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:56.219
Tim Nunamaker: Authorization to some client application to access my financial data whenever it wants to, or to access my medical records or social media posts whenever they want.

80
00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:00.229
Tim Nunamaker: That could create value for me as a user of that product.

81
00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:09.460
Tim Nunamaker: And in order for that to work, that application's probably going to need to read new data over time and fresh data.

82
00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:18.389
Tim Nunamaker: I think something that we've… Learned in working with a whole bunch of different types of personal data.

83
00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:20.540
Tim Nunamaker: at Open Data Labs in Invanna.

84
00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.920
Tim Nunamaker: Is most personal data is actually immutable.

85
00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:25.260
Tim Nunamaker: Meaning…

86
00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:37.970
Tim Nunamaker: you see new records all the time, but the records that you get typically aren't going to change. So that could be, you write that Slack post, you could edit it, but most of the time you don't.

87
00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:42.589
Tim Nunamaker: And… One of the decisions that we've made is that

88
00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:48.270
Tim Nunamaker: By differentiating between records that are append-only versus mutable.

89
00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:54.169
Tim Nunamaker: You can simplify the rules that client tooling and that the server

90
00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.189
Tim Nunamaker: Has to play by for the majority of the data.

91
00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:05.819
Tim Nunamaker: For example, if you can assume that this record is never going to change, then a client would never need to check again to see if it has changed.

92
00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:10.750
Tim Nunamaker: Some data types, are expected to change.

93
00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:15.650
Tim Nunamaker: my LinkedIn profile is going to change over the course of my career.

94
00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:19.870
Tim Nunamaker: And so, it's important that we can model mutability.

95
00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:28.439
Tim Nunamaker: One simplification that we could make in the protocol is to just say that all data is mutable, and that reduces

96
00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:35.109
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of the different rules that tools have to follow and that conformance requires.

97
00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:38.740
Tim Nunamaker: And so, this is kind of an optimization, and we're still weighing.

98
00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:43.520
Tim Nunamaker: What the right… Side of that scale is.

99
00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:53.010
Anna Kazlauskas: One perspective I'd be curious to hear from… Caleb, I don't want to put you on the spot, but I know you work on, kind of, data exports more from the platform side.

100
00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:57.630
Anna Kazlauskas: What do you think of, like, when you think about creating a personal data export.

101
00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:06.069
Anna Kazlauskas: Do you think of it as the historic dump of a user's data is pretty much immutable, or are you often, like, updating different aspects of it?

102
00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:11.389
Caleb: Usually immutable.

103
00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:12.550
Caleb: Cloud.

104
00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:18.130
Caleb: now I'm thinking about, this mutable state and running things through my head, so… No.

105
00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.120
Tim Nunamaker: Cool.

106
00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:31.270
Tim Nunamaker: So here's just a very simple manifest, so you can have an idea of what that looks like.

107
00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:36.740
Tim Nunamaker: We're basically defining What is this data source called?

108
00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:41.529
Tim Nunamaker: And what streams does it contain within those streams?

109
00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:45.300
Tim Nunamaker: What are the schemas, meaning what fields do they contain?

110
00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.160
Tim Nunamaker: And just some semantics that this is a…

111
00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:54.359
Tim Nunamaker: Mutable stream, like, here's where you can follow.

112
00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:58.990
Tim Nunamaker: New records over time by looking at this field as a cursor.

113
00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:05.169
Tim Nunamaker: Manifests actually can express a lot more semantics than this, and in PDPP,

114
00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:09.269
Tim Nunamaker: Some of them are optional, so the degree to which

115
00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:15.949
Tim Nunamaker: You can sort of make data filterable and rich and meaningful.

116
00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:24.339
Tim Nunamaker: Just depends on how hard you want to work to bubble up all of those different, sort of semantic details.

117
00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:33.090
Tim Nunamaker: maybe the most central design decision of PDPP is that…

118
00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:41.880
Tim Nunamaker: There is no canonical data model for what is an email, what is a social media post, what is a bank statement.

119
00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:49.540
Tim Nunamaker: I think people are probably familiar with things like schema.org and other protocols, including the Data Transfer Project.

120
00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:56.310
Tim Nunamaker: They're designed around A common definition of a particular type of data.

121
00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:01.360
Tim Nunamaker: We're making the choice to

122
00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:09.230
Tim Nunamaker: kind of meet the data where it is. So, if you imagine the long tail of data on the internet, a lot of it

123
00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:16.199
Tim Nunamaker: is probably fairly narrow in terms of the domain that it is defined in. There may not be

124
00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:23.930
Tim Nunamaker: a bunch of data providers that are all providing the same kinds of data. There could be differences between how different

125
00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:30.380
Tim Nunamaker: providers model different types of data. Some might have more rich data models than others.

126
00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:37.200
Tim Nunamaker: And I would say the key trade-off that this comes with is if you wanted to, for example.

127
00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:46.170
Tim Nunamaker: aggregate all of your transactions across different banks and credit card statements. You would then have to understand the schema for each one of those.

128
00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:55.109
Tim Nunamaker: However, we're not saying that you can't create a shared schema, and there's also nothing preventing you from creating

129
00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:58.350
Tim Nunamaker: Another shared schema that sort of sits on top of

130
00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:05.959
Tim Nunamaker: the different banks' schemas, and defines mappings across those. So you can imagine something like

131
00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:11.980
Tim Nunamaker: How Plaid sort of defined a common schema over existing

132
00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:18.189
Tim Nunamaker: provider-specific schemas, that could definitely be built on top of PDPP. And…

133
00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.679
Tim Nunamaker: I also think it's interesting that now that

134
00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:30.159
Tim Nunamaker: AI agents are becoming more ubiquitous, and they're able to work with schemas very easily. The cost of needing to understand

135
00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:32.579
Tim Nunamaker: You know, the next marginal schema.

136
00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:39.619
Tim Nunamaker: may be approaching zero over time, and so I think this is a bet that we feel pretty good about.

137
00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:42.990
Tim Nunamaker: And we have seen examples.

138
00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:46.890
Tim Nunamaker: I think, of where… trying to…

139
00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:52.380
Tim Nunamaker: Define in too much detail, or with too much abstraction.

140
00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:58.710
Tim Nunamaker: Like, this is what this type of data looks like. Can be a pretty big impediment to adoption.

141
00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:03.099
Tim Nunamaker: So we're just taking the opposite approach, and…

142
00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.660
Tim Nunamaker: I guess we'll see how that goes, but I feel good about that.

143
00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:17.030
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so now we're moving into the client experience. How does a client

144
00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:24.649
Tim Nunamaker: ask for data, which is in some resource server that is PDPP conformant, and

145
00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:28.609
Tim Nunamaker: I think it's important to recognize that,

146
00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:48.940
Tim Nunamaker: it actually starts with a client. It doesn't start with a user or the data source provider. The client is basically able to ask for access in sort of the same way that an OAuth client can ask for access to a Google user's Drive account or a Dropbox user's storage.

147
00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:53.190
Tim Nunamaker: This just reduces some of the friction in the process.

148
00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:58.639
Tim Nunamaker: So that the client application that wants access can initiate the process.

149
00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:03.409
Tim Nunamaker: And… What that looks like is a request.

150
00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:09.519
Tim Nunamaker: Where the client needs to specify not only what data

151
00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:14.310
Tim Nunamaker: do they want access to? Specifically, what streams and fields within

152
00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:22.670
Tim Nunamaker: The data, but also what, purpose are they requesting access for? What commitments are they making?

153
00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.439
Tim Nunamaker: About how the data will be treated.

154
00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:30.719
Tim Nunamaker: And… We'll talk a little bit more about this, I think.

155
00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:41.309
Tim Nunamaker: this is where we start to see PDPP is a bit opinionated about what it means for data to become portable, and we really want to

156
00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:48.569
Tim Nunamaker: make this work well for end users. And we think that with enough control.

157
00:21:49.360 --> 00:22:00.650
Tim Nunamaker: Authorization servers can do better than just, say, like, in OAuth, you just see something like, this application wants to use your account, and as a user, that could be a little bit confusing.

158
00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:09.250
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe one detail here to point out is… Clients can actually ask for

159
00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:13.420
Tim Nunamaker: optional access to data. So in this example.

160
00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:21.120
Tim Nunamaker: I need the top artists for this user's data. If they're interested in giving me tracks that they've saved.

161
00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:28.269
Tim Nunamaker: then I can use that in the experience that I'm creating for them. But if not, I can live without it.

162
00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.060
Tim Nunamaker: And then the user in the UI could indicate whether or not they want to share that data.

163
00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:36.860
Tim Nunamaker: So…

164
00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:40.160
Anna Kazlauskas: I think one question… Sara, I see you have a question. Do you want to jump in?

165
00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:44.889
Sara Saab: Sure, yeah, thank you. Is it okay to ask a question now, Tim?

166
00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:45.730
Sara Saab: Join us?

167
00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:46.440
Tim Nunamaker: I go for it.

168
00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:59.859
Sara Saab: So this was… I was dogfooding a little bit the other day, and I put this on GitHub as an issue as well. Like, the one… the one thing I wondered is, like, is there… is there a representation or semantics around inventory? Like,

169
00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:14.110
Sara Saab: Should the client know what the entire, like, corpus of streams available are by querying the resource server? Should there be a way to know that, or you have to come in knowing what you want?

170
00:23:14.570 --> 00:23:21.510
Tim Nunamaker: No, I think that's a great question. I think, it's a little bit of an open question that we want to figure out this month.

171
00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:27.560
Tim Nunamaker: And my default answer to that question is, a client shouldn't be able to know

172
00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:31.159
Tim Nunamaker: all of the data that a user has, that's data in itself.

173
00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:37.390
Tim Nunamaker: Like, you can imagine if your entire digital life is connected into some single personal server.

174
